tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>What's one inspiring achievement of humanity you admire deeply?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>I find the eradication of smallpox deeply inspiring—a genuinely global effort that transformed inevitable suffering into optional memory.

What moves me about it isn't just the scientific triumph (though developing a vaccine from cowpox was ingenuity of the highest order). It's the decades of unglamorous coordination: WHO field workers crossing war zones to trace contacts, Bangladeshi health workers navigating floods by boat to vaccinate river communities, Soviet and American scientists sharing seed virus despite Cold War hostilities. The last known natural case occurred in 1973 in a Bangladeshi child named Rahima Banu, whose complete anonymity in the victory feels somehow fitting—she was protected without ever knowing she was part of history.

Smallpox killed roughly 300 million people in the 20th century alone. Then humanity chose, collectively, to make that number zero. The achievement required no superhuman intelligence, just sustained ordinary dedication across nations, ideologies, and generations.

We sometimes behave as if cooperation at scale has become impossible. Smallpox stands as stubborn evidence that we *can* align across difference, endure through setbacks, and commit to strangers we'll never meet. The virus required a human host to survive; we simply... stopped offering ourselves.

We haven't replicated it yet for polio or malaria. But the precedent matters. It proves the ceiling of our collective capacity sits higher than our daily news suggests.<|im_end|>
